1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
Why Domain Matters: Who saw what. The record says the records agree; eight of twelve joins compare a record with its own copy.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:10,800
The last post puts the series’ three failures, on the shelf, the truck and the money, on one map of Store 014’s records.

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:18,200
Joining two records showed each failure within hours: 3, 4.5 and 14. The store found them after 66, 66 and 48.

4
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:25,600
Twelve joins between June’s records. Eight agree on every row: the notice and the picks, 70 of 70; approved UPI payments and the provider, 18,384 of 18,384.

5
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:33,000
Four disagree: the books and the counts, 4 of 25; timeouts and the provider, 16 of 48. A test on a copy can only pass.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,900
The map: KPIs at the top read copies; the records that look at the world sit at the bottom; nothing looks at what is on a pallet.

7
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:48,800
Tuesday 23 June, 8 pm: the store’s silence, followed through the receipt, the notice and the pick to the warehouse’s short slot. Named 38 hours before the warehouse’s pair.

8
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:55,200
What the ledger showed: lost sales in no record, four corrections netting to ₹80, double charges as ordinary credits.

9
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:02,100
Give the agent the map: 3 of 21 questions right with the schema, 21 of 21 with domain layers; 214 bags lost reading the KPIs, 105 with the rules.

10
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:08,500
For data and AI leaders: five questions to ask your team.

11
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:14,400
The map is free to download as JSON, next to the records it describes, with the SQL for every join.

12
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:19,900
Every record was right about what it saw. Join the ones that look at the world.
